module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.CompleteToricModel
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.FineSaturatedLogBlowup
import Sheaf.GroupTheory.SharpConeLatticeIntersection
import Sheaf.TraceFormula.RationalConeDualFinite

/-!
# Primitive facets of a sharp saturated cone

Source: ILO, Travaux de Gabber, Exposé VI 1.6(i)--(ii), printed p. 70;
the lattice-cone and facet preparation is the agent-formulated refinement recorded in
the DAG (blueprint: lem-trf-sharp-cone-primitive-facet-preparation).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open scoped BigOperators TensorProduct

namespace ToricMonoid

/-- Primitive normals, finite irredundant facet inequalities, and interior lattice witnesses
for the rational cone of a sharp fs monoid.
Source: the agent-formulated refinement of ILO VI 1.6(i)--(ii), printed p. 70,
recorded in the DAG (blueprint: lem-trf-sharp-cone-primitive-facet-preparation).
ILO supplies the model context, not this cone theorem; no proof is attributed to
Kato (1994), which has not been read.

Proof (the eleven steps of the node):
1. Apply `lem-trf-sharp-cone-lattice-intersection` to obtain the finite free lattice
   embedded in its rationalization, pointedness, spanning and `P = σ ∩ G`. Choose
   finite monoid generators. If `G = 0`, injectivity gives `P = 0`, hence `V = σ = 0`;
   the absence of nonzero forms gives no facets by `def-trf-complete-toric-model`.
2. Apply `lem-trf-rational-cone-dual-finite` to those generators. Delete zero forms
   and repeatedly delete redundant inequalities. Finiteness makes this terminate.
3. The sum `x₀` of the generators is strictly positive for every remaining form:
   otherwise that nonnegative form vanishes on all generators and hence on `V`.
   There is at least one inequality in positive dimension, since otherwise `σ = V`
   contradicts pointedness.
4. Irredundancy provides `yᵢ` violating only inequality `i`. Put `A = hᵢ(x₀) > 0`,
   `B = hᵢ(yᵢ) < 0`, and `α = -B/(A-B)`. The point
   `xᵢ = α x₀ + (1-α)yᵢ` has `hᵢ(xᵢ) = 0` and `hⱼ(xᵢ) > 0` for `j ≠ i`.
5. For `u ∈ ker hᵢ`, choose the positive rational epsilon equal to the minimum of
   `{1}` and the numbers `hⱼ(xᵢ)/(2|hⱼ(u)|)` with `j ≠ i`, `hⱼ(u) ≠ 0`.
   Both `xᵢ` and `xᵢ + εu` belong to the face. Their difference proves its span is
   `ker hᵢ`. Thus it is a facet, and the points `xᵢ` distinguish these facets.
6. For any facet given by `h`, a nonnegative expression in the generators has
   value zero under `h` precisely when only the zero-valued generators occur.
   Thus that facet is generated by the subfamily on which `h` vanishes.
7. Sum that subfamily to get `a`. Some `hᵢ(a)` is zero: otherwise choose `h(v) = -1`
   and perturb `a` by a positive rational epsilon, bounded by all relevant
   `hᵢ(a)/(2|hᵢ(v)|)`, contradicting nonnegativity of `h` on the cone.
8. Nonnegativity implies every summand in `hᵢ(a)` is zero. The facet is contained
   in `ker hᵢ`; equality of the hyperplane spans identifies it with facet `i`.
   This proves the claimed bijection and finiteness of all facets.
9. Clear the denominators of the values of `hᵢ` on a finite integral basis.
   The resulting integer homomorphism has image `bℤ`, where `b` is its least
   positive value (integer division proves this). Divide by `b` to get the
   surjective primitive normal, with positive rational scaling `N/b`.
10. Another primitive normal has the same rational kernel, hence is a scalar
    multiple. Evaluate at a lattice point with primitive value one to see the
    scalar is integral. Positivity on `x₀` makes it positive; surjectivity makes
    it one. Positive scaling also preserves the cone inequalities.
11. Clear the rational coordinates of each `xᵢ` in the lattice basis with a
    positive integer `Mᵢ`. Then `Mᵢ xᵢ ∈ σ ∩ G = P` by
    `lem-trf-sharp-cone-lattice-intersection`, and it has strictly positive value
    at every other facet. In rank one the unique facet is `{0}`, so both the
    rational and integral witness may be zero. -/
theorem sharp_cone_primitive_facet_preparation
    (P : Type u) [AddCommMonoid P]
    (hfs : AlgebraicGeometry.IsFineSaturatedMonoid (Multiplicative P))
    (hsharp : ∀ p : P, IsAddUnit p → p = 0) :
    (∃ r : ℕ, Nonempty
      (Module.Basis (Fin r) ℤ (Lattice P))) ∧
    Function.Injective (fun g : Lattice P ↦
      (1 : ℚ) ⊗ₜ[ℤ] g) ∧
    (∀ x : RationalSpace P, x ∈ cone P → -x ∈ cone P → x = 0) ∧
    (∀ x : RationalSpace P, ∃ a ∈ cone P, ∃ b ∈ cone P, x = a - b) ∧
    (∀ g : Lattice P, (1 : ℚ) ⊗ₜ[ℤ] g ∈ cone P ↔
      ∃ p : P, latticePoint p = g) ∧
    Set.Finite {F : Set (RationalSpace P) | IsFacet P F} ∧
    (∃ (ν : Facet P → Lattice P →+ ℤ)
      (h : Facet P → RationalSpace P →ₗ[ℚ] ℚ),
      (∀ F : Facet P,
        IsPrimitiveNormal F (ν F) ∧
        (∀ μ : Lattice P →+ ℤ, IsPrimitiveNormal F μ → μ = ν F) ∧
        IsRationalExtension (ν F) (h F) ∧
        F.val = {v | v ∈ cone P ∧ h F v = 0}) ∧
      (cone P : Set (RationalSpace P)) = {v | ∀ F : Facet P, 0 ≤ h F v} ∧
      (∀ F : Facet P, ∃ f : P, rationalPoint f ∈ F.val ∧
        ∀ H : Facet P, H ≠ F → 0 < ν H (latticePoint f)) ∧
      ((∀ g : Lattice P, g = 0) →
        (∀ p : P, p = 0) ∧
        (∀ v : RationalSpace P, v = 0) ∧
        (cone P : Set (RationalSpace P)) = ({0} : Set (RationalSpace P)) ∧
        IsEmpty (Facet P)) ∧
      ((∃ v : RationalSpace P, v ≠ 0) →
        ∃ (s m : ℕ) (I : Fin s → RationalSpace P →ₗ[ℚ] ℚ)
          (a : Fin m → P) (x₀ : RationalSpace P)
          (e : Fin s ≃ Facet P) (x : Fin s → RationalSpace P),
          0 < s ∧
          (∀ i : Fin s, I i ≠ 0) ∧
          (cone P : Set (RationalSpace P)) =
            {v | ∀ i : Fin s, 0 ≤ I i v} ∧
          (∀ i : Fin s, ∃ v : RationalSpace P,
            (∀ j : Fin s, j ≠ i → 0 ≤ I j v) ∧ I i v < 0) ∧
          (cone P : Set (RationalSpace P)) =
            ConvexCone.hull ℚ (Set.range (fun j : Fin m ↦ rationalPoint (a j))) ∧
          x₀ = ∑ j : Fin m, rationalPoint (a j) ∧
          (∀ i : Fin s, 0 < I i x₀) ∧
          (∀ i : Fin s, (e i).val =
            {v | v ∈ cone P ∧ I i v = 0}) ∧
          (∀ i : Fin s, x i ∈ cone P ∧ I i (x i) = 0 ∧
            (∀ j : Fin s, j ≠ i → 0 < I j (x i)) ∧
            Submodule.span ℚ ((e i).val) = LinearMap.ker (I i)) ∧
          (∀ i : Fin s, ∃ c : ℚ, 0 < c ∧ h (e i) = c • I i) ∧
          (∀ i : Fin s, ∃ (n : ℕ) (f : P), 0 < n ∧
            rationalPoint f ∈ (e i).val ∧
            (∀ H : Facet P, H ≠ e i → 0 < ν H (latticePoint f)) ∧
            rationalPoint f = n • x i))) := by
  sorry

end ToricMonoid
